\lispPackageChapter{livermore/animat-xcs}

The \texttt{livermore/animat-xcs} package is Woods-style maze navigation, the
classic multi-step XCS problem.  The animat sees the eight neighboring cells
and must walk to food.  Maze files live under
\texttt{data/animat-data/}.

\section{Classes}

\lispClassSubsection{animat-analyzer}

An environment and reinforcement program.  Construction reads
\texttt{file-name}: width, height, then one character per cell.
\texttt{*} is empty, \texttt{O} and \texttt{Q} are rocks, \texttt{F} and
\texttt{G} are food.

The situation is a $24$-element truth list: a three-bit sensor code for
each of the eight neighbors, counter-clockwise from north.  Edges wrap.

\lispClassSubsection{animat-experiment}

An experiment that uses an \texttt{animat-analyzer}.

\section{Methods}

\texttt{reset} places the animat on a random empty cell.
\texttt{execute-action} moves in one of eight directions; a rock is a no-op.
\texttt{get-reward} is $1000$ on food and $0$ otherwise.
\texttt{end-of-problem?} is true on food.

\lispFunctionSubsection{sensor-code}

The three-bit code for a cell symbol.

\lispFunctionSubsection{start-animat-experiment}

Builds an experiment from the maze in \lispVar{file-name} and starts it.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(start-animat-experiment \lispVar{file-name})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(start-animat-experiment
  (asdf:system-relative-pathname "livermore"
                                 "data/animat-data/Woods1.txt"))
\end{lstlisting}
